Evaluate $\displaystyle \int _{-1}^{1} \coth{x}\sinh{x}\,	extrm d x.$

question.worked_solution:
$$
\newcommand{\sech}{\mathop{\rm sech}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\csch}{\mathop{\rm csch}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\coth}{\mathop{\rm coth}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arsinh}{\mathop{\rm arsinh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arcosh}{\mathop{\rm arcosh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\artanh}{\mathop{\rm artanh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arsech}{\mathop{\rm arsech}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arcsch}{\mathop{\rm arcsch}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\arcoth}{\mathop{\rm arcoth}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\cosh}{\mathop{\rm cosh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\sinh}{\mathop{\rm sinh}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\csch}{\mathop{\rm csch}\nolimits}
\newcommand{\coth}{\mathop{\rm coth}\nolimits}
$$

First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity 

$$
\cot{x}=\dfrac{\cos{x}}{\sin{x}}.
$$

Using Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is

$$
\coth{x}=\dfrac{\cosh{x}}{\sinh{x}}.
$$

Now, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}{\int }_{-1}^{1}\operatorname{coth}⁡x\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x & ={\int }_{-1}^{1}\frac{\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\cdot \operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,\text{d}x \\  & ={\int }_{-1}^{1}\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\,\text{d}x \\  & =\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\  & =\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-1) \\  & =\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}}{2}-\frac{{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-(-1)}}{2} \\  & =\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1}}{2} \\  & =\frac{2e-2{e}^{-1}}{2} \\  & =e-{e}^{-1}.\end{aligned}
$$
